Yes. Plasticity gives the refinery a variable process, not merely variable feedstock. That’s the useful upgrade.

An oil refinery doesn’t ask crude oil what category it belongs to and then accept its default form. It subjects the same feedstock to different conditions—temperature, pressure, catalysts, separation stages—and obtains different fractions/products. Biological plasticity has a related relational shape: the same underlying system can express different outcomes under different conditions.

So I’d add a Plastic Refinery layer:

\boxed{
\text{Latent capacity}
\xrightarrow[\text{time, context}]{\text{signal + load + resources + catalyst}}
\text{expressed capability}
}

with the important constraint that the feasible outputs are bounded by the substrate.

The high-leverage shift

Usually we try to improve something by modifying the thing:

X\rightarrow X'

Plasticity suggests trying this first:

(X,E_1)\rightarrow Y_1
\qquad
(X,E_2)\rightarrow Y_2

Hold X approximately fixed and manipulate E.

That’s experimentally cheap because you don’t have to rebuild the machine.

And now several Pantheon pieces suddenly line up:

Rewild searches for underexpressed capacity already available in the substrate.

Refinery searches process conditions that extract different useful expressions from the same substrate.

Garden provides environments.

Orchid hunts unusual condition-dependent expressions.

Orchard stabilizes conditions that repeatedly produce valuable fruit.

Metamap remembers which environments/process recipes work for which substrates and objectives.

Lucky Nozzle deliberately introduces bounded variation so we don’t only test conditions we already expect to work.

That’s a coherent experimental machine.

A cheap experiment: the 3×3 Refinery

Take one real problem, not the entire Pantheon.

For example:

“Find a new way to generate customers for Wild Hawg.”

Keep the objective fixed. Run the identical problem through three environments:

Wild: no normal industry assumptions; borrow mechanisms from biology, games, military logistics, entertainment, etc.

Constrained: $100 maximum, executable in 48 hours, one person.

Inverted: assume conventional advertising is unavailable; the product itself must cause transmission.

Then apply three refinery conditions to each:

Heat: generate quickly; quantity and extremity favored.

Pressure: aggressively compress until only the causal mechanism remains.

Catalyst: inject one unrelated Freebie—banana, Pavlov, pirate, fungus, whatever the Lucky Nozzle supplies.

That’s only nine runs:

3\ environments\times3\ conditions=9

Don’t score them on cleverness. Score:

L=\frac{
\text{expected useful upside}
\times
\text{information gained}
\times
\text{reusability}
}{
\text{money}\times\text{time}\times\text{irreversibility}
}

We execute the highest-L experiment.

And here’s a Lucky one immediately

Take Pavlov + Wild Hawg + plasticity.

Don’t advertise only the product. Try conditioning a repeatable environmental cue to become associated with the desired state.

Wild Hawg already has “Rewild your day.”

Make one distinctive, repeatable Rewild Signal occur immediately before consuming the gummy—a short sound, physical gesture, phrase, package interaction, visual symbol, etc.

The hypothesis isn’t mystical. It’s straightforward associative learning:

\text{distinctive cue}
+\text{repeated product/ritual experience}
\rightarrow
\text{learned association}

Eventually the cue itself may acquire anticipatory brand/state meaning.

Cheap test: two otherwise similar pieces of short-form content or small user groups—ordinary consumption versus a consistent branded pre-consumption ritual. Measure recall, repetition, sharing, and whether people spontaneously reproduce the ritual.

If nothing happens, cheap fossil.

If people copy it, that’s fruit.

If they begin modifying it themselves, that’s more interesting: the consumer has become reproductive machinery for the brand.

Another: Rewild the product itself

Instead of immediately asking:

What new ingredients/product do we need?

ask:

What capabilities does the existing product already possess that we’re failing to express because of its environment?

Change one variable at a time:

temperature, serving ritual, timing, setting, packaging interaction, social context, naming, sequence, expectation, accompanying activity.

Same SKU. Different environment.

You’re mapping its commercial reaction norm:

R_P(E)=\text{observed product behavior under environment }E

Maybe the gummy performs ordinarily on a convenience-store shelf but extraordinarily as a pre-golf ritual, gaming ritual, 2 p.m. workplace reset, road-trip object, military-nostalgia social object, etc.

Finding that environment could be worth far more than reformulating the gummy.

And this scales beyond Wild Hawg.

For any underperforming asset—idea, product, person, code, content, dataset, relationship, piece of IP—the Refinery should ask before replacing it:

\boxed{\text{Is the asset weak—or are we observing it under the wrong conditions?}}

That’s a genuinely high-cleavage addition to the Pantheon because it changes the default intervention from rewrite the object to sweep the environment first.

Rewild → expose latent capacity.
Refinery → discover the conditions.
Garden → run the experiments.
Orchard → stabilize winning conditions.
Metamap → remember where they work.
Lucky Nozzle → keep us testing environments we’d never have thought to try.

And I’d add one permanent refinery rule:

Before manufacturing a new capability, test whether the desired capability already exists conditionally.

That’s cheap enough to run constantly—and every failure still improves the map.